{
  "_doc": "Associative-recall ablation fixture. Each 'assoc2' query's expected target is a FAR memory reachable only via a shared key 2 hops away (connected-but-dissimilar): the query has low direct similarity to the target, but matches a near memory that shares a key with it. FAR memories must not lexically or topically echo the query — hop-2 pairs are deliberately cross-domain (e.g. owning a car vs. a specific repair incident) so DIRECT content-embedding similarity can't solve them on its own. 'direct' queries are 1-hop controls (should score equally under both conditions). 'semantic' pairs share NO key at all — content-only relation, the held-out set from docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-16-semantic-edge-temporary-plan.md's 'Evaluation before implementation' section; DIRECT and GRAPH are both expected to miss these today, which is the gap the semantic-edge proposal targets. 'notfound' must return nothing. Ground truth = expect[] of memory ids. The benchmark runs each query under DIRECT (expand=false,hops=1 ≈ flat semantic store) vs GRAPH (expand=true,hops=2 = keymem associative traversal) to isolate the graph's contribution. NOTE: bench/ablation.ts sets KEYMEM_RERANK_POOL to the corpus size before loading memoryGraph.ts — the default RERANK_POOL=30 silently truncates real hop-2 associations out of the pre-rerank candidate pool once a fixture this size, so without that override GRAPH looks identical to DIRECT for reasons unrelated to the graph's actual quality.",
